---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import audit from '../../data/a11y-audit.json';

const violationCount = audit.violations.length;

const headings = [
  { id: 'audit', label: 'Automated audit' },
  { id: 'aria', label: 'ARIA attributes' },
  { id: 'keyboard', label: 'Keyboard' },
  { id: 'focus', label: 'Focus' },
  { id: 'screen-readers', label: 'Screen readers' },
  { id: 'color-contrast', label: 'Color contrast' },
  { id: 'reduced-motion', label: 'Reduced motion' },
  { id: 'no-javascript', label: 'Without JavaScript' },
];
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Accessibility"
  description="How ARC UI is checked against WCAG 2.1 AA, and how its components handle keyboard, focus, screen readers, contrast, reduced motion and pages without JavaScript."
  activeSlug="accessibility"
  headings={headings}
  breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Accessibility' }]}
>
  <div class="a11y-page">
    <h1 class="page-title">Accessibility</h1>
    <p class="page-desc">
      ARC UI targets WCAG 2.1 Level AA. Components render native elements where they
      exist, such as a real <code>&lt;button&gt;</code>, and set ARIA roles where they don't.
    </p>

    <section id="audit">
      <h2 class="docs-heading">Automated audit</h2>
      <arc-divider align="left" variant="line-gradient" style="max-width:48px;margin-bottom:var(--space-md)"></arc-divider>
      <p class="docs-text">
        Every component page, live demos included, is checked with
        <a href="https://github.com/dequelabs/axe-core">axe-core</a> against the rendered
        DOM, shadow roots included, in both themes. CI fails on any serious or critical
        violation, color contrast included, and no rules are switched off. Guide pages, the
        home page and the Theme Synthesizer aren't part of the audit.
      </p>
      <div class="audit-stats">
        <div class="audit-stat">
          <span class="audit-stat__value">{audit.pagesAudited}</span>
          <span class="audit-stat__label">Pages audited</span>
        </div>
        <div class="audit-stat">
          <span class="audit-stat__value">2</span>
          <span class="audit-stat__label">Themes audited (dark and light)</span>
        </div>
        <div class="audit-stat">
          <span class="audit-stat__value">{violationCount}</span>
          <span class="audit-stat__label">Violations, any severity</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="docs-text audit-meta">
        Last audit: {audit.date} · axe-core {audit.axeVersion} · run it yourself with
        <code>pnpm audit:a11y</code>
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="aria">
      <h2 class="docs-heading">ARIA attributes</h2>
      <arc-divider align="left" variant="line-gradient" style="max-width:48px;margin-bottom:var(--space-md)"></arc-divider>
      <p class="docs-text">
        Components set their own roles. <code>arc-dialog</code> renders a native
        <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code> opened as a modal, and <code>arc-tabs</code> sets
        <code>tablist</code>, <code>tab</code> and <code>tabpanel</code>.
        ARIA attributes you put on a host element mostly stay on the host. They don't reach
        the control inside the shadow root. <code>arc-icon-button</code> is the exception:
        it moves a host <code>aria-label</code> onto its inner button. <code>arc-button</code> doesn't forward
        <code>aria-label</code>, and <code>arc-input</code> can't pass
        <code>aria-describedby</code> into its shadow root, so the first two snippets below
        show what doesn't work. For an icon-only button, use <code>arc-icon-button</code>
        and give it a <code>label</code>. <code>arc-input</code> has no prop for a description.
        <code>arc-alert</code> announces errors and
        warnings assertively and success politely by default. Its <code>live</code> prop
        overrides that.
      </p>
      <div class="docs-code"><arc-code-block language="html" code={`<!-- Labels -->
<arc-button aria-label="Close dialog">
  <arc-icon name="x"></arc-icon>
</arc-button>

<!-- Descriptions -->
<arc-input
  label="Password"
  aria-describedby="pw-hint"
></arc-input>
<span id="pw-hint">Must be at least 8 characters</span>

<!-- Live regions -->
<arc-alert aria-live="polite" variant="success">
  Changes saved successfully
</arc-alert>`}></arc-code-block></div>
    </section>

    <section id="keyboard">
      <h2 class="docs-heading">Keyboard</h2>
      <arc-divider align="left" variant="line-gradient" style="max-width:48px;margin-bottom:var(--space-md)"></arc-divider>
      <p class="docs-text">
        These tabs are live. <kbd>Tab</kbd> to them and try the keys each one describes.
        The table below lists the keys for common components.
      </p>
      <div class="kbd-demo">
        <arc-tabs>
          <arc-tab label="Arrows"><kbd>Arrow Left</kbd> and <kbd>Arrow Right</kbd> move between tabs. The panel follows focus.</arc-tab>
          <arc-tab label="Home / End"><kbd>Home</kbd> jumps to the first tab, <kbd>End</kbd> to the last.</arc-tab>
          <arc-tab label="Tab"><kbd>Tab</kbd> leaves the tablist. The whole widget is one tab stop.</arc-tab>
        </arc-tabs>
      </div>
      <div class="token-table-wrap">
        <table class="token-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Component</th><th>Keys</th><th>Action</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Button</td><td><kbd>Enter</kbd> / <kbd>Space</kbd></td><td>Activate</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Dialog</td><td><kbd>Escape</kbd></td><td>Close</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Tabs</td><td><kbd>Arrow Left/Right</kbd>, <kbd>Home</kbd> / <kbd>End</kbd></td><td>Switch tab</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Accordion</td><td><kbd>Enter</kbd> / <kbd>Space</kbd></td><td>Toggle panel</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Select</td><td><kbd>Arrow Up/Down</kbd></td><td>Move between options</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Select</td><td><kbd>Enter</kbd></td><td>Select option</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Select</td><td><kbd>Escape</kbd></td><td>Close the list</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Toggle</td><td><kbd>Space</kbd></td><td>Toggle on/off</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Checkbox</td><td><kbd>Space</kbd></td><td>Toggle checked</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Drawer</td><td><kbd>Escape</kbd></td><td>Close</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Tooltip</td><td><kbd>Escape</kbd></td><td>Dismiss</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="focus">
      <h2 class="docs-heading">Focus</h2>
      <arc-divider align="left" variant="line-gradient" style="max-width:48px;margin-bottom:var(--space-md)"></arc-divider>
      <p class="docs-text">
        There are two focus indicator tokens. <kbd>Tab</kbd> through these to compare them.
      </p>
      <div class="focus-demo-row">
        <button class="focus-card focus-card--ring">
          <code>--focus-ring</code>
          <span class="focus-card__hint">1px ring</span>
        </button>
        <button class="focus-card focus-card--glow">
          <code>--focus-glow</code>
          <span class="focus-card__hint">Ring and glow</span>
        </button>
      </div>
      <p class="docs-text">
        Buttons and similar controls show focus on keyboard navigation
        (<code>:focus-visible</code>), not on a mouse click, so clicking the cards above shows
        nothing. Text fields show it on click as well. <code>arc-dialog</code> and a modal
        <code>arc-drawer</code> trap focus while open. A <code>no-modal</code> drawer doesn't,
        and opening one leaves focus where it was.
      </p>
      <div class="docs-code"><arc-code-block language="css" code={`/* Customize focus styles globally */
:root {
  --focus-ring: 0 0 0 2px var(--bg-deep),
                0 0 0 4px var(--accent-primary);
  --focus-glow: var(--focus-ring),
                0 0 16px rgba(var(--accent-primary-rgb), 0.3);
}`}></arc-code-block></div>
    </section>

    <section id="screen-readers">
      <h2 class="docs-heading">Screen readers</h2>
      <arc-divider align="left" variant="line-gradient" style="max-width:48px;margin-bottom:var(--space-md)"></arc-divider>
      <p class="docs-text">
        ID references can't cross a shadow root, so a <code>&lt;label for&gt;</code> outside
        a component can't point at the field inside it.
      </p>
      <ul class="docs-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Slotted content</strong> stays in the light DOM, where screen readers
          reach it directly.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Form controls</strong> take a <code>label</code> attribute, which the
          component ties to its inner field. Set it and the field is announced with its label.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <div class="docs-code"><arc-code-block language="html" code={`<!-- Use slot for screen-reader-accessible content -->
<arc-card>
  <h3>Account Settings</h3>
  <p>Manage your profile and preferences.</p>
</arc-card>

<!-- Use label attribute for form elements -->
<arc-input label="Email address"></arc-input>
<arc-select label="Country">
  <option value="us">United States</option>
  <option value="uk">United Kingdom</option>
</arc-select>`}></arc-code-block></div>
    </section>

    <section id="color-contrast">
      <h2 class="docs-heading">Color contrast</h2>
      <arc-divider align="left" variant="line-gradient" style="max-width:48px;margin-bottom:var(--space-md)"></arc-divider>
      <p class="docs-text">
        The audit above measures rendered contrast in both themes. With the default tokens:
      </p>
      <ul class="docs-list">
        <li>
          <code>--text-primary</code> on <code>--bg-deep</code> is above 15:1 in the dark
          theme and above 13:1 in light.
        </li>
        <li>
          <code>--text-muted</code> and <code>--text-ghost</code> meet the 4.5:1 ratio for
          normal text in both themes.
        </li>
        <li>
          The feedback colors (<code>--color-success</code>, <code>--color-warning</code>,
          <code>--color-error</code>, <code>--color-info</code>) pass in both dark and
          light modes.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <arc-alert variant="warning">If you override colors, check the contrast yourself. The <a href="/synth">Theme Synthesizer</a> shows the ratio for each text and background pair in both themes.</arc-alert>
    </section>

    <section id="reduced-motion">
      <h2 class="docs-heading">Reduced motion</h2>
      <arc-divider align="left" variant="line-gradient" style="max-width:48px;margin-bottom:var(--space-md)"></arc-divider>
      <p class="docs-text">
        With <code>prefers-reduced-motion: reduce</code> set, every transition and animation
        inside a component finishes almost instantly.
      </p>
      <div class="docs-code"><arc-code-block language="css" code={`/* Built into ARC UI components */
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  *,
  *::before,
  *::after {
    animation-duration: 0.01ms !important;
    animation-iteration-count: 1 !important;
    transition-duration: 0.01ms !important;
  }
}`}></arc-code-block></div>
      <p class="docs-text">
        Your own components get the same rule if they include <code>tokenStyles</code>. A
        collapsed animation runs once, immediately, and the element then shows its
        <em>un-animated</em> styles, so a reduced-motion user sees whatever the base styles
        say. Put the resting state in the base styles and let the keyframes move away from it.
        A two-frame sprite that swaps layers by opacity shows both frames stacked if both are
        visible by default. Set <code>opacity: 0</code> on the second frame in the base styles,
        as below, and a reduced-motion user sees only the first frame.
      </p>
      <div class="docs-code"><arc-code-block language="css" code={`.frame-b { opacity: 0; }            /* resting state: frame A only */
.frame-a { animation: swap-a 800ms steps(1) infinite; }
.frame-b { animation: swap-b 800ms steps(1) infinite; }

@keyframes swap-a { 50% { opacity: 0; } }
@keyframes swap-b { 50% { opacity: 1; } }`}></arc-code-block></div>
    </section>

    <section id="no-javascript">
      <h2 class="docs-heading">Without JavaScript</h2>
      <arc-divider align="left" variant="line-gradient" style="max-width:48px;margin-bottom:var(--space-md)"></arc-divider>
      <p class="docs-text">
        Web Components need JavaScript to upgrade. Until that happens (a failed script, a
        slow network, JS disabled), a custom element is an unknown tag rendering only its
        light DOM.
      </p>
      <p class="docs-text">
        For links, an <code>href</code> on the custom element itself
        <strong>cannot</strong> produce a working link without JavaScript. The attribute is
        on the element, and the real <code>&lt;a&gt;</code> only exists in a shadow root that
        never gets created. Slot a real anchor instead. It works as a link before the element
        upgrades.
      </p>
      <div class="docs-code"><arc-code-block language="html" code={`<!-- Works without JS, and before the element upgrades -->
<arc-button variant="primary">
  <a href="/get-started">Get started</a>
</arc-button>

<arc-navigation-menu>
  <arc-nav-item><a href="/docs">Docs</a></arc-nav-item>
  <arc-nav-item><a href="/pricing">Pricing</a></arc-nav-item>
</arc-navigation-menu>

<!-- Needs JS to become a link at all -->
<arc-button variant="primary" href="/get-started">Get started</arc-button>`}></arc-code-block></div>
      <p class="docs-text">
        On upgrade the component uses that anchor as its control and doesn't render a second
        one, so the accessibility tree has one link. The <code>href</code> form still works.
        Use it in app shells where JavaScript always runs, and use the slotted form on public
        pages. Slotted anchors work in <code>arc-button</code>,
        <code>arc-icon-button</code>, <code>arc-link</code>, <code>arc-card</code>,
        <code>arc-nav-item</code>, <code>arc-breadcrumb-item</code>, and
        <code>arc-sidebar-link</code>.
      </p>
      <p class="docs-text">
        The stylesheet hides components until they upgrade, but only when scripting is
        available, so with JavaScript disabled your content stays visible.
      </p>
      <div class="docs-code"><arc-code-block language="css" code={`/* Shipped in base.css */
@media (scripting: enabled) {
  :is(arc-button, arc-link /* … */):not(:defined) { opacity: 0; }
}`}></arc-code-block></div>
      <p class="docs-text">
        Components that render their content from properties, such as
        <code>arc-feature-card</code> (<code>heading</code> and <code>description</code>),
        have no light DOM to fall back to and stay blank without JavaScript. Treat them as
        enhancement-only, or <a href="/docs/server-rendering">server-render them</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="see-also">
      <h2 class="docs-heading">See also</h2>
      <arc-divider align="left" variant="line-gradient" style="max-width:48px;margin-bottom:var(--space-md)"></arc-divider>
      <ul class="see-also__list">
        <li><a href="/docs/server-rendering">Server rendering</a>: content in the HTML before JavaScript runs</li>
        <li><a href="/docs/getting-started">Getting started</a>: installation and setup</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </div>
</DocsLayout>

<style>
  /* Token table */
  .token-table-wrap {
    border: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    overflow-x: auto;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-md);
  }

  .token-table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
  }

  .token-table th {
    text-align: left;
    font-family: var(--font-accent);
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    letter-spacing: 2px;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--text-ghost);
    font-weight: 600;
    padding: var(--space-sm) var(--space-md);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);
    background: var(--bg-elevated);
  }

  .token-table td {
    padding: var(--space-sm) var(--space-md);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);
    color: var(--text-secondary);
  }

  kbd {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    background: var(--bg-elevated);
    padding: var(--space-xs);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    border: 1px solid var(--border-default);
    box-shadow: 0 1px 0 var(--border-default);
  }

  /* Live keyboard demo */
  .kbd-demo {
    padding: var(--space-md);
    border: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    background: var(--bg-card);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-md);
  }

  /* Focus demos */
  .focus-demo-row {
    display: flex;
    gap: var(--space-lg);
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-md);
  }

  .focus-card {
    width: 180px;
    height: 90px;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: var(--space-xs);
    border: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    background: var(--bg-card);
    cursor: pointer;
    outline: none;
    transition: box-shadow var(--transition-base), border-color var(--transition-fast);
    font-family: inherit;
    color: inherit;
  }

  .focus-card code {
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    color: var(--text-secondary);
  }

  .focus-card__hint {
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    color: var(--text-ghost);
  }

  .focus-card--ring:focus-visible {
    box-shadow: var(--focus-ring);
    border-color: var(--accent-primary);
  }

  .focus-card--glow:focus-visible {
    box-shadow: var(--focus-glow);
    border-color: var(--accent-primary);
  }

  .audit-stats {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(160px, 1fr));
    gap: var(--space-md);
    margin: var(--space-lg) 0;
  }

  .audit-stat {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-xs);
    padding: var(--space-md);
    border: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    background: var(--bg-card);
  }

  .audit-stat__value {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-2xl);
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--text-primary);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  .audit-stat__label {
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    color: var(--text-secondary);
  }

  .audit-meta {
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    color: var(--text-secondary);
  }

</style>
